{{also|Astrakhan}}
==English==
[[File:Fur coat (AM 2016.47.1-1).jpg|thumb|'''astrakhan''' fur coat]]

===Etymology===
Named after the city of [[Astrakhan]] in Southern Russia.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/æstɹəˈkæn/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Closely-curled black or [[grey]] [[fleece]] of very young [[karakul]] [[lamb]]s from [[Astrakhan]].
# [[cloth|Cloth]] resembling the above mentioned [[fur]], often made from [[wool]] and [[mohair]] and used for [[trimmings]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|author=[[w:Sir Arthur Conan Doyle|Sir Arthur Conan Doyle]]|title=[[w:A Scandal In Bohemia|The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes]]|passage=Heavy bands of '''astrakhan''' were slashed across the sleeves and fronts of his double-breasted coat {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=[[w:James Joyce|James Joyce]]|title=[[w:The Dead (short story)|The Dead]]|passage=He was dressed in a long green overcoat with mock '''astrakhan''' cuffs and collar and wore on his head an oval fur cap.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1924|author=[[w:Richard Connell|Richard Connell]]|title=[[w:The Most Dangerous Game|The Most Dangerous Game]]|passage=He was dressed in uniform—a black uniform trimmed with gray '''astrakhan'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1929|author=[[w:Virginia Woolf|Virginia Woolf]]|title=[[w:A Room of One's Own|A Room of One's Own]]|publisher=Penguin Books, paperback edition|page=33|passage=Was the cavalry officer slim and elegant, and dressed in '''astrakhan'''?}}

